WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.120
Can one GABA input hold back excitation, and then help it a few milliseconds later?

2
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.870
The shunt was tied exactly at rest.

3
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.470
But the GABA rope's end depends on the chloride gradient.

4
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:18.293
Suppose it is tied at minus −55 mV.

5
00:00:18.913 --> 00:00:22.033
That is above rest, but below threshold at minus −50

6
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:26.263
A 10 nS GABA rope opens in our resting cell.

7
00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:28.183
Where does the marker settle?

8
00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:37.557
At minus −60 halfway, because the two ropes are equally strong.

9
00:00:38.457 --> 00:00:40.437
This GABA input depolarized the cell.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.980
Now strong excitation drives the voltage above minus −55 toward threshold.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.500
Which way does the GABA rope pull?

12
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:59.613
Down, back toward minus −55 And its conductance enlarges the bottom line.

13
00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:02.273
So it opposes further depolarization.

14
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:04.873
Then the GABA conductance closes.

15
00:01:05.893 --> 00:01:08.373
Excitation arrives a few milliseconds later.

16
00:01:09.273 --> 00:01:11.333
Does it start nearer threshold, or farther?

17
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:22.743
Nearer. The conductance has gone, but the depolarization it left decays with τ.

18
00:01:23.523 --> 00:01:25.143
The later input starts higher up.

19
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:30.417
At each moment, an open synapse pulls the voltage toward its own reversal potential.

20
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.100
So this GABA current depolarizes while the voltage is below its end.

21
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.600
It hyperpolarizes once the voltage is above it.

22
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.760
Current direction alone does not say whether GABA helps firing.

23
00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.260
For that, compare the whole response with and without it.

24
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:51.143
With steady inputs, it can never carry a cell sitting below threshold past

25
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:56.123
threshold. The new balance point stays between the old one and minus −55

26
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:58.487
Brief inputs are different.

27
00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:03.887
In a model of our cell, one fast EPSP alone stopped just short of threshold.

28
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:09.577
With the GABA conductance already open, it started from minus −60 and just crossed.

29
00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:14.127
The open GABA conductance made the EPSP itself smaller.

30
00:02:14.687 --> 00:02:16.747
But the 5 mV head start won.

31
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:22.837
Gulledge and Stuart studied this in layer 5 pyramidal neurons, in cortex slices from

32
00:02:22.837 --> 00:02:24.357
rats three to four weeks old.

33
00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:30.037
GABA responses depolarized the cells from rest, though the GABA reversal potential

34
00:02:30.037 --> 00:02:31.357
was below threshold.

35
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:36.610
GABA at the cell body inhibited excitation arriving at the same time.

36
00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.530
It made later excitation more likely to fire the cell.

37
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:44.503
GABA on the dendrites was excitatory, whatever its timing.

38
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.267
The balance point offers a reading of the timing result.

39
00:02:49.207 --> 00:02:52.987
Likely, the conductance had closed while some depolarization remained.

40
00:02:53.667 --> 00:02:57.727
This does not make GABA excitatory in adult cortex in general.

41
00:02:58.587 --> 00:03:01.847
In that preparation, its sign depended on timing and site.

42
00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:04.037
The rope's end can also move.

43
00:03:04.897 --> 00:03:09.317
When chloride enters faster than transporters can remove it, the GABA reversal

44
00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:10.697
potential creeps upward.

45
00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:14.367
GABA pulls the voltage toward its reversal potential.

46
00:03:15.307 --> 00:03:19.887
Whether that helps or hinders firing depends on timing, site and the other inputs.
